# Clean Architecture

## Layer Responsibilities

- `domain`: business rules, entities, typed errors, value objects, and pure
  policies.
- `application`: service orchestration and high-level workflows.
- `infrastructure`: FFI, native protocol, repositories, and external-system
  implementations.
- `core` or `shared`: cross-cutting utilities used by multiple layers.
- `lib/odbc_fast.dart`: curated public API barrel for package consumers.

## Dependency Direction

- `domain` may depend only on `domain` and `core/shared`.
- `application` may depend on `domain` and `core/shared`.
- `infrastructure` may depend on `domain`, `application` contracts, and
  `core/shared` when implementing outer concerns.
- No inner layer may import an outer layer.
- Public package exports should flow through `lib/odbc_fast.dart`, not through
  ad-hoc scattered entrypoints.

## Boundary Rules

- Keep `domain` free of FFI, transport, isolate, and persistence details.
- Keep `application` focused on orchestration, not wire formats or native
  pointer concerns.
- Keep FFI, buffer parsing, bindings, and native lifecycle management in
  `infrastructure`.
- Define abstractions close to the business rule that owns them.
- Bind concrete implementations at composition-root boundaries, not inside the
  domain.

## Design Heuristics

- Keep use-case boundaries cohesive and split orchestration from mapping when
  concerns compete.
- Prefer small, focused interfaces over broad "god" contracts.
- Depend on abstractions at boundaries, not concrete implementations.
- Keep external-data normalization at the boundary before values spread inward.

## SOLID and Composition

- Apply single responsibility to files, classes, services, and use cases.
- Prefer composition and delegation over inheritance for sharing behavior.
- Extend behavior through new implementations or collaborators instead of
  editing stable paths when that keeps the design simpler.
- Keep implementations substitutable for their contracts; avoid hidden
  preconditions that break callers using an abstraction.
- Split broad interfaces when consumers are forced to depend on unused members.
- Keep dependency inversion at boundaries: inner policies define contracts,
  outer infrastructure provides implementations.

## Smells to Fix

- Domain importing native bindings, protocol, or transport code.
- Application code parsing raw buffers or dealing with pointers.
- One class handling validation, orchestration, persistence, and mapping.
- Large interfaces that force clients to depend on unused members.
- Public APIs exported accidentally instead of deliberately through the barrel.
